{"Below are given details of the game in which Cafferty gained a valuable, if
lucky, point in his game with L. W. Barden in the Premier Reserves Major
tournament at Hastings, the principal section after the Premier" (BH Wood)} 1.
c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. e4 c5 4. e5 Ng8 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Be2 d6 7. exd6 Bxd6 8. d4 Nf6
9. d5 exd5 10. cxd5 Ne5 11. O-O Nxf3+ 12. Bxf3 O-O 13. Bg5 Bf5 14. Re1 h6 15.
Bh4 g5 16. Bg3 Bxg3 17. hxg3 Re8 18. Qb3 Qd7 19. d6 Qxd6 20. Qxb7 Rxe1+ 21.
Rxe1 Rb8 22. Qxa7 Rxb2 23. Rd1 Qe5 24. Nd5 Nxd5 25. Bxd5 Be6 26. Bxe6 Qxe6 {
"Barden should now have reconciled himself to the fact that he cannot succeed,
but, even though short of time, tries to fish in troubled waters. He blunders
horribly!" (BH Wood)} 27. Qxc5 Qe2 28. Rd8+ Kh7 29. Qd4 $4 (29. Qf5+ $16) 29...
Rb1+ 30. Kh2 Qh5+ {Sources: Bernard Cafferty; Birmingham Post, 6 January 1960,
p12} 0-1

