{"The Evans Gambit" (Leonard Barden, Guardian, 18/10/1963). Robert Fischer,
the young US champion, told me a few years ago that he regarded all the
gambits resulting from 1 e4 e5 unsound, be they King's, Scotch, Danish, or
Evans. Now it seems that Fischer has changed his mind to some extent. In the
recent New York State championship, which he won with 7 out of 7, Fischer was
twice prepared to play the Evans, but his opponents "declined" with the Two
Knights' Defence.  One of the new ideas in the Evans is illustrated in this
week's game played in the semi-finals of the world postal team championship.
The notes are based on the winner's."} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4
Bxb4 5. c3 Be7 ({This is thought to be a solid defence. In practice, though,
Black's best antidote to the gambit is} 5... Ba5 6. d4 d6 7. Qb3 Qd7 8. dxe5
Bb6 {and whether White plays 9 exd6, 9 0-0 or 9 Nbd2, Black replies 9...Na5
eliminating the powerful bishop (LWB)}) 6. d4 Na5 ({Not} 6... exd4 $2 7. Qb3 {
(LWB)}) 7. Bd3 exd4 ({A recommendation of Euwe but sounder is} 7... d6 8. dxe5
Bg4 {(LWB)}) 8. cxd4 d5 9. Qa4+ $1 (9. exd5 Qxd5 10. Nc3 Bb4 $1) 9... Nc6 {
Recommended by Konstantinopolsky in Shakhmatny Biulleten 9-01959.} (9... c6 10.
exd5 ({If 9...c6} 10. Bd2 {favours White (LWB)}) 10... Qxd5 11. Nc3 $14 {
Sokolsky}) 10. exd5 (10. e5 $5 {Ostroverkhov-Flammiger}) 10... Qxd5 11. Nc3 (
11. O-O {was played in some unconvincing games, met by 11...Bd7 and 11...Nf6.})
11... Qa5 $2 ({According to Keres, the equalising line is:} 11... Bb4 12. Bd2
Bxc3 (12... Qa5 $5 {Larry Evans}) 13. Bxc3 Nge7 {gave chances for K to escape
centre (notes Cafferty, FSCH 8/63)}) 12. Qxa5 Nxa5 13. Nb5 $14 {White has some
advantage since, despite the pawn minus, he has active play with his minor
pieces. The continuation was instructive:} Bd8 (13... Bd6 14. O-O {or Nxd6+
with good attacking chances}) 14. Bf4 c6 $4 15. Nd6+ $1 Ke7 $2 (15... Kf8 {
was critical.}) 16. O-O Be6 17. Rfe1 Kf8 {(White threatened 18 Ng5 followed by
19 Ndxf7 - LWB)} 18. Bf5 Be7 ({Not} 18... Bxf5 $2 19. Re8#) 19. Bxe6 fxe6 20.
Rxe6 Nf6 21. Nf5 Nd5 $2 {(Missing White's strong reply but his position was
already bad - LWB)} 22. Rxe7 $1 $18 Nxe7 ({Or} 22... Nxf4 23. Rae1 $1 {with
mating possibilities - LWB}) 23. Bd6 Kf7 24. Bxe7 Ke6 $2 25. g4 g6 26. Re1+ Kd7
27. Ne5+ Kc7 28. Bd6+ Kb6 $8 29. Nd7+ Kb5 30. Rb1+ Kc4 ({King excursions of
this type are normally, as here, forced marches rather than exercise sprees. If
} 30... Ka4 31. Nc5+ Ka3 32. Ne3 {and mates by 33 Nc2 - LWB}) 31. Ne3+ $1 Kd3 (
31... Kxd4 32. Rd1+ Ke4 33. Kg2) 32. Nc5+ (32. Nc5+ {For if} Ke2 33. Kg2 $1 Z0
{and to postpone the finish} 34. Rb2+ Ke1 35. Nd3# {Black must give up another
piece by 33...Nc4. A lively and well-played game by Cafferty, illustrating how
to carry through an attack after the queens are exchanged - LWB}) 1-0

