[SourceQuality "1"]

{Norman Stephenson comments: "At the start of the game I had unintentionally
kept Bernard waiting five minutes or so ... I'd been playing blitz for an hour
or more against Mazitis in a side room for a shilling a game and had lost
track of the time."} 1. d4 e6 2. e4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 6. Bd3 Bd7
7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Qxe5 11. Re1 Qd6 12. Nb5 Bxb5 13.
Bxb5+ Kd8 14. Qh5 Qe7 15. Bf4 a6 16. Ba4 Nf6 17. Qe5 b5 18. Bb3 Qd7 19. Rec1
Ne8 20. Qd4 Qa7 21. Qd2 Bc5 22. g4 b4 23. Rc2 Nf6 24. Rxc5 Qxc5 25. Rc1 {
Norman Stephenson comments: "Black's OK here because he can give up the queen
on 'c7' (25...Qb6!) but it's going to need some care against a marauding white
queen (fortunately, his remaining bishop would not be so active) ... with only
7 minutes to reach move 36, I was happy enough to accept Bernard's draw offer
!" Source: Bernard Cafferty and Norman Stephenson. Norman's version of the
score gives 1 e4 e6 2 d4 d5 rather than Bernard's 1 d4 e6 2 e4 d5.} 1/2-1/2

