1. g3 d5 2. Nf3 Bg4 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. d4 Qd7 5. h3 Bf5 6. Bf4 h6 {Waste of time.
It was not necessary to provide a retreat for his f5 bishop, as Nh4 by White
was not a threat.} 7. c4 Nf6 ({His best chance was to go in for complications
by} 7... dxc4 8. d5 Nb4 9. Nd4 g5 10. Be3 Bg7 {threatening ...Bxd4. Black is
already suffering from blocking his own c-pawn. Could he now play ...c6 his
game would be more solid.}) 8. Ne5 Qe6 ({Not} 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 {and White
wins the d-pawn.}) 9. Nc3 Nxe5 ({Almost as good as resigning already. The
pressure on his d-pawn had become almost intolerable in any event: if he plays
} 9... dxc4 {White wins by} 10. d5 $1 {The opening had been so complicated
that the game had already gone half the three hours allotted for the first 30
moves.}) 10. dxe5 Nd7 11. cxd5 Qb6 12. O-O g5 13. Be3 Qxb2 {It is remarkable
how often Cafferty secures a powerful "pawn steam-roller" from a diffident
type of opening such as 1.g3, 2.Nf3, 3.Bg2 and how well he handles it. Care is
imperative and a fierce thrust only when the situation quite justifies it;
otherwise enemy forces can break through and exploit the big open spaces
behind the original attacker's pawns.} 14. Bd4 Bg7 15. e4 Bg6 16. Rb1 Qa3 17.
Rxb7 ({There may have been a quicker win by} 17. e6 Nf6 ({not} 17... Ne5 18. f4
{winning the pinned knight}) 18. exf7+ {and 19.e5}) ({or} 17. Nb5 Qa5 18. Bc3
Qb6 {(otherwise Nxc7+ would win a rook)} 19. Nd6+ {winning a queen for a rook
and knight; releasing the pressure a little, however. Black is certainly in a
desperate fix.}) 17... O-O 18. f4 gxf4 19. gxf4 Rfc8 20. Rb3 {To switch this
rook into the attack on Black's king.} Qa6 21. f5 Bh7 22. f6 exf6 23. exf6 Nxf6
24. e5 Ne8 ({Not} 24... Nd7 25. Qg4 {and 26.e6.}) 25. Ne4 Rab8 26. Rg3 Rb4 27.
Nf6+ Kh8 28. Nxe8 Rxe8 29. Rxg7 Kxg7 30. e6+ f6 {Otherwise "resigns"!} 31.
Bxf6+ Kg8 ({Or} 31... Kg6 32. Qc2+ {, mating.}) 32. Qh5 Rf8 33. e7 $1 Qb6+ 34.
Kh1 Rb1 35. Qg4+ {Source: Birmingham Daily Post, 12 October 1965 p22.
Contributed by Gerard Killoran, 16 Feb 2019. Notes by BH Wood, "based on
comments supplied by the winner."} 1-0

