1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d4 exd4 5. O-O Bc5 6. e5 d5 7. exf6 dxc4 8.
Re1+ Be6 9. Ng5 Qd5 10. Nc3 Qf5 11. Nce4 O-O-O 12. g4 Qe5 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. Bg5
Bb4 (14... g6 15. f7 (15. Nxc5 $2 Qxc5 16. Bh6 e5 $145 (16... Qd5 $145 $1 17.
f7 Ne5 $1 $17 {Keene-Divinsky, Havana ol 1966}) 17. f7 Qd5 {occurred in the
1890s, according to Estrin, after which 14 Bg5 was forgotten until the Prasad
game revived it.}) 15... Rd7 $2 (15... Be7 $145 {see games in database}) 16.
Bf6 {Prasad-Kalyanasundaram, the game which caused a sensation and revived 14
Bg5}) (14... h6 15. Nxc5 (15. fxg7 hxg5 16. gxh8=Q Rxh8 17. Ng3 Qd6 $1 $11 18.
Qe2 $140 d3 $1 19. Qxe6+ Qxe6 20. Rxe6 Nd4 {Estrin in Central Chess Club
Bulletin, circa 1968}) 15... Qxc5 (15... Qxg5 16. fxg7 $1 {and White remains
the whole exchange up with the h-file closed}) 16. fxg7 hxg5 17. gxh8=Q Rxh8
18. Rxe6 {and Black's Q cannot get to a threatening square (Cafferty-Sombor,
Bognor Regis 1965)}) 15. f4 $1 (15. fxg7 Rhg8 {Beisser-Niemi}) 15... Qb5 (15...
Qa5 {Murrey-Asaritis}) 16. fxg7 Rhg8 17. Bxd8 Bxe1 18. Bf6 Bb4 19. Ng5 Qd5 20.
Qe2 $16 {(To here, Cafferty translation of Estrin article in Chess 557-8 (June
1968) - the game continues...)} e5 21. fxe5 d3 22. Qe4 Bc5+ 23. Kh1 Qxe4+ 24.
Nxe4 Bd4 25. cxd3 Bxe5 26. g5 cxd3 27. Rd1 Rxg7 28. Bxg7 Bxg7 29. Rxd3 h6 30.
gxh6 Bxh6 31. h4 Nb4 32. Rg3 Nxa2 33. h5 Bc1 34. Rg6 {Source: BC scoresheets/
scorebooks} 1-0

